\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{Motivation and aim}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A few nonlinear equations carry a model neuron from rest to tonic
spiking and to bursting.
\citet{§1.2, pp. 8–10}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
treats the neuron as a dynamical system. The Hodgkin–Huxley model
on p. 8 takes as its state the membrane potential #{V} together with
the gating variables #{n}, #{m}, and #{h} for the persistent potassium
current and the transient sodium current, and its law is a system of
ordinary differential equations: the next moment depends on the input
and on the state at the present moment. The same section shows rest, periodic
spiking, and bursting. Bursting there is a rhythmic return from a spiking mode to rest
and back again, and a slow conductance can bring that return about.
\citet{§3.1, p. 49}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
reads the solutions of such equations as curves in phase space, and uses
that geometry to sort the solutions a model neuron can have: whether it
fires, for which parameter values it oscillates, and how the frequency
depends on those values.}

\p{By contrast, a fixed map from a stimulus to a response misses the
dependence on the state at which the stimulus arrives. For one model
neuron,
\citet{§3.3.2, p. 53}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
records that a small perturbation of voltage from rest decays back to
rest, while a sufficiently large perturbation continues to increase and
makes an action potential.
\citet{Figure 3.1a, pp. 50–51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
draws those two outcomes in the excitable Morris–Lecar regime.
\citet{§1.1.2, p. 3}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
sets the classical picture, one voltage above which a shock produces
a spike, beside two experiments. In one, brief strong shocks of several
amplitudes depolarize the membrane to several values. In the other,
long weak pulses of several amplitudes produce a slow depolarization
and then a spike. The section finds no voltage that separates the
trials which spike from the trials which do not, and it asks where, in
the slow rise, the depolarization ends and the spike begins. We take
the ongoing activity of brain networks to be a background process, and
a stimulus to be an arrival into that process. The books cited in this
section treat single model neurons. The network sentence is a view of
the notes.}

\p{The notes therefore take the state and its evolution as the object
of study. A recorded voltage is one coordinate of that state. The same
stimulus can arrive at different states and leave different traces.
The examples come from neuron models, and the statements concern flows,
equilibria, and periodic orbits. Which evolution of the state produces
the voltage that a recording keeps?}

\p{Control enters in two senses, and the notes keep the senses separate.
In one sense the brain is a controller. Motor control moves the body,
and homeostasis holds internal quantities near the values the organism
maintains. In the other sense the brain is an object that can be
observed, steered, or triggered from outside. Stimulation and an
interface that both records and stimulates act on the brain in that
second sense.}

\p{A single control description of the whole brain would blur
distinctions that the phenomena keep separate. The notes therefore
separate measurement, state, behavior, and intervention. Measurement
is the record, since a recording does not hand over the state.
The state is the point that evolves. Behavior is an observable output
of that state. Intervention is an input to the dynamics, and it includes
sensory arrival and stimulation applied from outside.}

\p{Structure and development supply the physical origin of these
dynamics. The account runs from the way a neuron is built, through the
development of a general cell into a neuron, to the growth and refinement
of synapses from infancy to adulthood, and to the behavior that growth
produces.}

\p{Therefore the mathematics remains the focus. Biology supplies the
worked examples, and the notes propose no biological theory. A method
familiar in neuroscience is recovered from a mathematical statement.
Each chapter keeps a remark that relates the two, and a worked example
that carries the recovery on a neuron model.}
